1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,380
Mumbai to Ahmedabad.

2
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:03,240
Five hundred and eight kilometres.

3
00:00:03,260 --> 00:00:03,960
By car?

4
00:00:03,980 --> 00:00:05,960
About eight hours, three toll plazas, and

5
00:00:05,980 --> 00:00:07,120
one fight about the AC.

6
00:00:07,680 --> 00:00:09,679
By India's bullet train, opening from twenty

7
00:00:09,699 --> 00:00:10,300
twenty seven?

8
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:11,140
About two hours.

9
00:00:11,460 --> 00:00:13,300
That's shorter than one cricket innings.

10
00:00:13,320 --> 00:00:14,640
So today, we're taking a ride.

11
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:15,800
Seven coaches.

12
00:00:16,040 --> 00:00:16,840
Seven secrets.

13
00:00:17,510 --> 00:00:19,410
Each coach explains one trick that lets

14
00:00:19,430 --> 00:00:21,291
a train cruise at three hundred and twenty

15
00:00:21,311 --> 00:00:22,330
kilometres an hour.

16
00:00:22,670 --> 00:00:24,710
And to prove how smooth it is, I've balanced

17
00:00:24,730 --> 00:00:26,790
a ten rupee coin on this tray table.

18
00:00:27,110 --> 00:00:28,830
If it falls, I owe you a chai.

19
00:00:29,110 --> 00:00:31,410
I'm your guide, and yes, I also check tickets.

20
00:00:31,970 --> 00:00:32,830
Please take your seats.

21
00:00:33,150 --> 00:00:33,950
The doors are closing.

22
00:00:36,600 --> 00:00:37,320
Coach two.

23
00:00:37,340 --> 00:00:38,500
Why is the nose so long?

24
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:40,440
Because at three hundred kilometres an hour,

25
00:00:40,620 --> 00:00:42,720
air stops behaving like air, and starts

26
00:00:42,740 --> 00:00:43,960
behaving like a wall.

27
00:00:44,340 --> 00:00:46,120
A flat front would slam into it.

28
00:00:46,480 --> 00:00:49,360
A long, pointy nose slices through it instead.

29
00:00:49,820 --> 00:00:50,620
Tunnels are worse.

30
00:00:51,760 --> 00:00:53,840
A blunt train rams into a tunnel like a

31
00:00:53,860 --> 00:00:55,800
pump, squeezing the air ahead, and the far

32
00:00:55,820 --> 00:00:56,720
end goes boom.

33
00:00:57,180 --> 00:00:59,040
Japan fixed this by copying a bird.

34
00:00:59,320 --> 00:01:01,280
The kingfisher dives into water with barely

35
00:01:01,300 --> 00:01:03,280
a splash, so engineers gave their train

36
00:01:03,300 --> 00:01:04,100
a beak.

37
00:01:04,120 --> 00:01:04,820
Less boom.

38
00:01:04,840 --> 00:01:05,620
Less noise.

39
00:01:05,640 --> 00:01:06,440
Less electricity.

40
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:08,440
Nature did the research, for free.

41
00:01:11,733 --> 00:01:12,433
Coach three.

42
00:01:12,453 --> 00:01:13,653
There's no engine up front.

43
00:01:13,893 --> 00:01:14,693
No diesel.

44
00:01:14,713 --> 00:01:15,513
No smoke.

45
00:01:15,673 --> 00:01:16,953
The power hangs above you.

46
00:01:16,973 --> 00:01:19,093
Twenty five thousand volts runs through

47
00:01:19,113 --> 00:01:20,293
a wire over the track.

48
00:01:20,773 --> 00:01:22,993
On the roof, a springy arm called a pantograph

49
00:01:23,013 --> 00:01:25,273
presses up against that wire, sliding along

50
00:01:25,293 --> 00:01:27,153
at three hundred kilometres an hour without

51
00:01:27,173 --> 00:01:27,973
letting go.

52
00:01:28,773 --> 00:01:29,673
And here's the trick.

53
00:01:29,873 --> 00:01:32,393
There's no single locomotive dragging everyone.

54
00:01:33,133 --> 00:01:34,893
Motors are spread across the coaches, so

55
00:01:34,913 --> 00:01:36,493
the whole train pushes together.

56
00:01:36,513 --> 00:01:38,913
Like a tug of war team where everyone is

57
00:01:38,933 --> 00:01:39,733
actually pulling.

58
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:43,760
Coach four.

59
00:01:43,780 --> 00:01:45,840
Normal trains ride on a bed of stones.

60
00:01:46,440 --> 00:01:48,040
Bullet trains ride on concrete.

61
00:01:48,540 --> 00:01:51,020
The rails sit on solid concrete slabs, a

62
00:01:51,040 --> 00:01:54,000
Japanese system called J slab, used in India

63
00:01:54,020 --> 00:01:55,240
for the very first time.

64
00:01:55,620 --> 00:01:57,500
No stones to shift, so no bumps.

65
00:01:58,660 --> 00:02:00,120
And more than ninety percent of the route

66
00:02:00,140 --> 00:02:02,220
runs on a bridge, ten to fifteen metres

67
00:02:02,240 --> 00:02:03,040
above the ground.

68
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:06,260
So no level crossings, no cows, and no uncle

69
00:02:06,280 --> 00:02:07,800
on a scooter trying his luck.

70
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:10,460
Oh, and near Mumbai, the train dives under

71
00:02:10,480 --> 00:02:12,280
the sea, through a seven kilometre tunnel

72
00:02:12,300 --> 00:02:13,220
below Thane Creek.

73
00:02:13,240 --> 00:02:13,800
Under.

74
00:02:13,820 --> 00:02:14,120
The.

75
00:02:14,140 --> 00:02:14,940
Sea.

76
00:02:18,667 --> 00:02:19,347
Coach five.

77
00:02:19,367 --> 00:02:20,087
Who's driving?

78
00:02:20,107 --> 00:02:21,627
At three hundred and twenty, you cover almost

79
00:02:21,647 --> 00:02:22,947
a football field every second.

80
00:02:23,067 --> 00:02:24,907
So a driver can't read signals on poles

81
00:02:24,927 --> 00:02:25,387
outside.

82
00:02:25,407 --> 00:02:27,027
They'd blur past like a missed call.

83
00:02:27,167 --> 00:02:28,947
Instead, the signals come inside, on a screen

84
00:02:28,967 --> 00:02:29,687
in the cab.

85
00:02:29,707 --> 00:02:31,567
A system called ATC keeps telling the train

86
00:02:31,587 --> 00:02:33,067
the safe speed for the track ahead.

87
00:02:33,387 --> 00:02:35,127
Go too fast, and the train slows itself

88
00:02:35,147 --> 00:02:35,667
down.

89
00:02:35,687 --> 00:02:36,407
Politely.

90
00:02:36,427 --> 00:02:37,227
Without asking.

91
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:41,600
Coach six.

92
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:42,480
Fun fact.

93
00:02:42,620 --> 00:02:44,800
Bullet trains are terrible at stopping quickly.

94
00:02:45,200 --> 00:02:47,440
From top speed, an emergency stop still

95
00:02:47,460 --> 00:02:49,120
takes around four kilometres.

96
00:02:49,140 --> 00:02:51,440
So the computer plans every stop way ahead.

97
00:02:51,740 --> 00:02:53,560
And when the train brakes, its motors flip

98
00:02:53,580 --> 00:02:55,780
into generators, and push electricity back

99
00:02:55,800 --> 00:02:56,600
into the wire.

100
00:02:57,180 --> 00:02:58,140
Now, earthquakes.

101
00:02:58,160 --> 00:03:00,360
Twenty eight seismometers along the route

102
00:03:00,380 --> 00:03:02,460
listen for the first, faster tremor.

103
00:03:02,820 --> 00:03:04,800
The moment they sense it, the power is cut,

104
00:03:04,820 --> 00:03:07,140
and every train in the area brakes, ideally

105
00:03:07,160 --> 00:03:09,440
before the strong shaking even arrives.

106
00:03:13,033 --> 00:03:13,733
Last coach.

107
00:03:13,753 --> 00:03:13,873
So.

108
00:03:13,893 --> 00:03:14,733
A beak for a nose.

109
00:03:14,953 --> 00:03:15,753
Power from a wire.

110
00:03:16,293 --> 00:03:17,213
Motors spread across the train.

111
00:03:17,413 --> 00:03:18,933
Concrete track on a bridge in the sky.

112
00:03:19,493 --> 00:03:20,213
Signals inside the cab.

113
00:03:20,233 --> 00:03:21,953
And seismometers listening for earthquakes.

114
00:03:22,233 --> 00:03:23,653
All of that, so you can go from Mumbai to

115
00:03:23,673 --> 00:03:25,313
Ahmedabad faster than one long movie.

116
00:03:25,593 --> 00:03:27,273
And the ten rupee coin on the tray table?

117
00:03:27,413 --> 00:03:28,133
Still standing.

118
00:03:28,153 --> 00:03:28,953
I don't owe you a chai.

119
00:03:29,033 --> 00:03:29,793
We have arrived.

120
00:03:29,813 --> 00:03:30,753
Please collect your belongings.

121
00:03:31,073 --> 00:03:31,873
Mind the gap.
